course: dictionary
lesson: 13273
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «The film was full of blood and gore, so Mia covered her eyes.» Blood and gore go together, and a scary film is the clue. Here gore means thick blood from a wound.
explain: Gore can also be a verb, an action word. «The angry bull gored the farmer with its horn.» Gored tells you what the bull did: it wounded the farmer with a horn. Ask yourself: is the word a thing, or is it something someone or something does?
explain: Gore has a third meaning, used in sewing. «Grandma sewed a gore into the skirt so it would swing wider.» A gore is a triangle of cloth. Sewing and skirt are the clues. Always test your meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: Gore is a noun meaning thick blood from a wound, especially when there is a lot of it.
fact: To gore is a verb meaning to wound someone with a horn or tusk.
fact: A gore is a triangular piece of cloth sewn into a skirt, sail or umbrella to make it wider.
fact: «Blood and gore» is often used about violent films, which may be too scary for young children.
fact: When gore means blood, it is uncountable, so we say «too much gore», not «a gore».
exercise: «The farm story told how the bull gored the farmer.» What does gored mean here? | To cook a meal | To paint a picture | To sing loudly | *To wound with a horn
exercise: «The film had too much gore for Sam's little brother.» What does gore mean? | *Thick blood from a wound | A small triangle of cloth | A type of sweet | A loud noise
exercise: «Aunt Jo sewed a gore into the sail to make it wider.» What is a gore in this sentence? | Thick blood from a wound | A cook who makes sails | *A triangle of cloth | A kind of animal
test: «The angry goat gored the boy, and he needed help from the nurse.» What does gored mean? | To wash clothes in a river | *To wound with a horn or tusk | To hide under a bed | To hug someone tightly
test: «The horror film had a lot of blood and gore, so it was rated for adults only.» Which meaning fits? | A soft, warm blanket | A triangle of cloth | To wound with a horn | *Thick blood from a wound
exam: «The skirt had a gore at each side so it could swing.» Which meaning of gore is used here? | To wound with a horn or tusk | A loud and angry noise | *A triangular piece of cloth | Thick blood from a wound
